\documentclass{article}
\title{Task-Adaptive Initialization for Few-Shot Learning}
\author{Anon}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We study task-adaptive initialization for few-shot classification and propose a method that conditions the initial parameters on task statistics.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Few-shot learning asks models to adapt to new tasks from a handful of examples. Model-agnostic meta-learning finds an initialization that adapts quickly, but it uses the same initialization for every task. We argue that task statistics should modulate the initialization.
\section{Method}
We compute channel-wise mean and variance of the support features and feed them into a small hypernetwork that outputs per-layer scale and shift for the initialization. The hypernetwork is trained jointly with the meta-objective.
\section{Experiments}
On two few-shot benchmarks our method improves 5-way 1-shot accuracy over the MAML baseline by 3 points, with 4 percent extra parameters. Gains are larger on tasks that are far from the training distribution.
\end{document}
